    {
      "id": "3d97102b-cca4-4eb2-819a-d64f2291eb6f",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Findings\n\n**When do managers sub? Late \u2014 and mostly when they're already losing.**\n\n1. **Subs live in the final half hour.** Across 24,726 substitution\n   events, 75% arrive between minutes 60 and 89, peaking at 70\u201379\n   (28%). First-half subs are rare \u2014 under 10% happen before minute 50.\n\n2. **The five-sub era is visible but coverage-limited.** Sub event data\n   exists only for StatsBomb-covered competitions (~4,000 matches,\n   concentrated in recent seasons): 2023/24 shows ~0.9 subs per\n   team-match and 44% of matches with at least one sub \u2014 consistent\n   with the five-sub rule. Earlier seasons show near-zero coverage,\n   not near-zero subs \u2014 the event importer simply doesn't reach them.\n\n3. **First-sub timing tracks the scoreline, not the result.** Among\n   team-matches with substitution data, teams whose first sub came\n   before minute 55 lost **51.7%** of the time; teams holding out until\n   80+ won **54.6%**. The gradient is monotone-ish \u2014 but this is the\n   reactive signature: trailing teams sub early *because* they're\n   trailing. With no score-state data we cannot separate \"early subs\n   cause losses\" from \"losing causes early subs\" \u2014 and the honest answer\n   is almost certainly the latter.\n\n4. **What we can't see:** who was subbed *off* (the event's player is\nthe player coming on), whether the sub changed the result, and sub\npatterns for ~97% of all matches in the dataset. This is a proof of\nconcept on a small window, not a league-wide law.\n\n**Verdict:** substitutions are football's most reactive stat \u2014 they\nmeasure desperation more than tactics. The timing curve (peak 70\u201379)\nis real; the win/loss gradient is a mirror of the scoreboard, not a\nlever on it.",
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": []
    }
